.TH I2C 3
.SH NAME
i2c \- I2C buses, as files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #J /dev

.BI /dev/i2c N ctl
.BI /dev/i2c N data
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I I2c
is a polled master, with 7-bit addresses, on the Raspberry Pi 5's header:
bus 0 on GPIO 0 and 1, bus 1 on GPIO 2 and 3.
The pins become I2C's at the first open.
.PP
Writing the control file sets:
.TP
.BI addr " n"
the device to talk to.
.TP
.BI subaddress " n"
that each transfer starts with the file offset, in
.I n
bytes, high first, and that a read then uses a repeated start: how most
register and EEPROM devices are read.
.TP
.BI speed " n"
100000 or 400000.
.PP
Reading it returns those settings.
Reads and writes of the data file are one transfer each with the device at
.IR addr ,
of at most 4096 bytes.
.PP
An address nothing answers gives the error
.BR "no device" ,
which is how to scan a bus.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/bcm2712/devi2c.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpio (3),
.IR spi (3)
